[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The scene features two simple, white matte ceramic plates arranged side-by-side on an aged, dark oak wood surface with visible grain and natural use marks. The camera angle is a 3/4 overhead view, capturing the full volume of the dish from a height that reveals all components clearly while maintaining a shallow depth of field with the second plate softly blurred in the background. Soft, natural side light streams from a window on the left, casting subtle, gentle shadows and highlighting the moist, succulent texture of the snapper. On each plate, a generous base of nutty, cooked farro is mixed with quartered artichoke hearts, vibrant halved cherry tomatoes that show slight roasting, and glossy, dark pitted kalamata olives. Resting beside the farro, not on top, is a tender, pearly white red snapper fillet that flakes naturally. Thin, translucent lemon slices are draped over the fish, with a few drops of olive oil and herb specks visible. Props include a rumpled, off-white linen napkin tucked slightly under one plate, a small wooden spoon resting near the edge, and one squeezed, exhausted lemon half placed naturally on the wooden surface. The scene is lived-in and authentic, featuring small traces of the cooking process like a stray herb leaf and a tiny smear of oil on the wood. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The plates show minor, realistic imperfections like a small sauce drip on the rim. Warm, golden-hour color temperature creates a cozy, inviting atmosphere. No plastic sheen, no unnatural symmetry. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. The image is indistinguishable from a professional cookbook photograph."
]
